Return a flushed picture's buffer to the pool in single-threaded decoding

Applies to OpenH264 2.6.0 (codec/decoder/plus/src/welsDecoderExt.cpp) as
openh264-sys2 0.9.8 vendors it under upstream/; `patch -p1 -d upstream`.

Without threads, every picture of a stream above the baseline profile is held
for reordering (BufferingReadyPicture takes a reference on it), and one whose
picture order count lsb steps by more than one is held until the next picture
arrives. FlushFrame hands the held picture over with a NULL context, and the
release functions return the reference only through a context or m_pPicBuff,
which only the threaded path sets. Each flush therefore keeps one buffer out
of the pool of num_ref_frames + 2, and PrefetchPic fails once the pool is
empty: a caller that flushes after each access unit to avoid the one-frame
delay stops decoding at the fifth picture. Passing the single context, as
ReorderPicturesInDisplay does, returns the reference.

--- a/codec/decoder/plus/src/welsDecoderExt.cpp
+++ b/codec/decoder/plus/src/welsDecoderExt.cpp
@@ -934,11 +934,12 @@
     }
   }
   if (bEndOfStreamFlag && m_sReoderingStatus.iNumOfPicts > 0) {
+    PWelsDecoderContext pCtx = m_iThreadCount <= 1 ? m_pDecThrCtx[0].pCtx : NULL;
     if (!m_sReoderingStatus.bHasBSlice) {
-      ReleaseBufferedReadyPictureNoReorder (NULL, ppDst, pDstInfo);
+      ReleaseBufferedReadyPictureNoReorder (pCtx, ppDst, pDstInfo);
     }
     else {
-      ReleaseBufferedReadyPictureReorder (NULL, ppDst, pDstInfo, true);
+      ReleaseBufferedReadyPictureReorder (pCtx, ppDst, pDstInfo, true);
     }
   }
   return dsErrorFree;
